.PP
No free form takes a lazy.
A lazy reads a path with its member \f[CR]lazy.at_path\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]encoded\f[R] is a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R], so a
\f[CR]std::string\f[R] or one element of a \f[CR]cbor::sequence\f[R]
goes in directly.
A path is a string literal for the compile\-time forms.
A run\-time path is a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] that
\f[CR]jsonpath::compile<T>\f[R] parses once.
The query owns its data and is applied to any number of items.
\f[CR]T\f[R] of a \f[CR]compile\f[R] that is not \f[CR]nodelist\f[R]
needs a singular path, else the result is \f[CR]not_singular_query\f[R].
\f[CR]nodelist\f[R] is \f[CR]std::vector<lazy>\f[R].
A compile\-time path that is not valid leaves no form to call: the
compiler reports that no function matches.
.PP
\f[CR]cbor::schema<T>::at_path\f[R] reads a member of a message that
\f[CR]cbor::schema<T>\f[R] wrote (schema.md).
Its path names members and indexes only.
On a \f[CR]std::array\f[R] or a \f[CR]std::vector\f[R] of
\f[CR]std::uint8_t\f[R] or \f[CR]std::byte\f[R], \f[CR][n]\f[R] there
reads one byte.
A path of this page that indexes a byte string gives
\f[CR]not_indexable\f[R].
.PP
A wrong use that the compiler cannot see throws
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R]:
.IP \(bu 2
a null or empty owner in a form that takes an owner;
.IP \(bu 2
a \f[CR]cbor::lazy{}\f[R] that holds no item, in every form that takes a
lazy.
.SS A view and its owner
\f[CR]T\f[R] of a view is one of \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R],
\f[CR]std::span<std::byte const>\f[R] and \f[CR]cbor::typed_array\f[R].
A view type needs an owner of the bytes.
A view type with a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] alone does not compile.
No form copies the input bytes: the form with an rvalue
\f[CR]std::string\f[R] moves them.
.PP
The owner beside a view is a \f[CR]std::shared_ptr<void const>\f[R].
The view is a borrowed range of \f[CR]char\f[R], for example a
\f[CR]std::string_view\f[R].
The function does not check that the owner holds the bytes.
A \f[CR]std::string\f[R] beside the owner does not compile, because it
is not a borrowed range.
.PP
A \f[CR]ref\f[R] compares and reaches its value as
\f[CR]std::optional\f[R] does: \f[CR]*name == \(dqann\(dq\f[R] and
\f[CR]name\->size()\f[R] read the view, \f[CR]name == \(dqann\(dq\f[R]
compares it, and two \f[CR]ref\f[R] compare their values.
\f[CR]ref<cbor::typed_array>\f[R] has no \f[CR]==\f[R], as
\f[CR]cbor::typed_array\f[R] has none.
.SS 2. Any item as a node
The forms without a type give the node as a \f[CR]cbor::lazy\f[R].
The node can be any CBOR item: a map, an array, a tag.
Read it on with a step, \f[CR]get\f[R], \f[CR]type()\f[R] or
\f[CR]clone()\f[R] (runtime.md).
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// {\(dqa\(dq: [1, 2, 3]}\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$.a\(dq>();          \f[I]// the node of [1, 2, 3]\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$.a[*]\(dq, cbor::nodelist>();   \f[I]// the nodes of 1, 2 and 3\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const e = cbor::jsonpath::compile<cbor::nodelist>(\(dq$.a[?\(at > 1]\(dq);
l.at_path(*e);               \f[I]// the nodes of 2 and 3, path at run time\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$.b\(dq, cbor::nodelist>();      \f[I]// no node, no error\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] with a node type gives one node.
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] with \f[CR]nodelist\f[R] gives the nodelist, also for
a singular path and for an empty nodelist.
It gives no error when a selector finds nothing.
