.PP
The parts in detail:
.IP \(bu 2
A name in dot form starts with a letter, \f[CR]_\f[R] or a byte of 0x80
or more, and goes on with these or digits.
A name in brackets is in single or double quotes, with the escapes of
RFC 9535 2.3.1 and of EDN.
.IP \(bu 2
An index counts from 0.
\f[CR][0]\f[R] is the first element and \f[CR][\-1]\f[R] the last.
.IP \(bu 2
An index is an integer of at most 16 digits without leading zeros, from
\-(2\(ha53 \- 1) to 2\(ha53 \- 1.
An integer of 16 digits or less outside this range is
\f[CR]invalid_path\f[R].
\f[CR]$[01]\f[R] is \f[CR]invalid_path\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Any other item in brackets is an EDN literal, and the path reads it as a
map key (section 7).
So \f[CR]$[\-0]\f[R] is the key 0, and an integer of 17 digits or more
is a key.
On an array, such a key is \f[CR]not_indexable\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
A slice is \f[CR]start:end:step\f[R].
Each part can be absent.
Step 0 selects nothing.
A slice of a node that is not an array selects nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]&&\f[R] binds more tightly than \f[CR]||\f[R].
\f[CR]!\f[R] negates an existence test or a group in parentheses.
.IP \(bu 2
A literal in a filter is an EDN literal.
A JSON number, a string, \f[CR]true\f[R], \f[CR]false\f[R] and
\f[CR]null\f[R] are EDN literals too.
.IP \(bu 2
Blanks are allowed before a segment and inside brackets.
A blank before \f[CR]$\f[R] or after the last segment is
\f[CR]invalid_path\f[R].
.PP
A wildcard on a map selects the values.
A name selects only from a map.
An index selects from an array, and from a map with an integer key
(section 7).
.SS 5. Comparisons
A comparison is \f[CR]==\f[R], \f[CR]!=\f[R], \f[CR]<\f[R],
\f[CR]<=\f[R], \f[CR]>\f[R] or \f[CR]>=\f[R].
Each side is a singular query, a literal or a function.
A side that is a query that is not singular is \f[CR]invalid_path\f[R],
for example \f[CR]$[?\(at.* == 1]\f[R].
.PP
