.IP
.EX
\f[I]// [{\(dqt\(dq: \(dqabc\(dq}, {\(dqt\(dq: [1, 2]}, {\(dqt\(dq: 5}]\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$[?length(\(at.t) == 3].t\(dq, cbor::nodelist>();    \f[I]// the node of \(dqabc\(dq\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$[?count(\(at.t[*]) == 2].t\(dq, cbor::nodelist>();  \f[I]// the node of [1, 2]\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$[?value(\(at..t) == 5].t\(dq, cbor::nodelist>();    \f[I]// the node of 5\f[R]
cbor::jsonpath::compile<cbor::nodelist>(\(dq$[?match(\(at.t, \(aqa.c\(aq)]\(dq);  \f[I]// invalid_path\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A compile\-time path with \f[CR]match\f[R] or \f[CR]search\f[R] does not
compile:
\f[CR]is_valid_path_v<\(dq$[?match(\(at.t, \(aqa.c\(aq)]\(dq>\f[R] is
false.
A function that is not well\-typed (RFC 9535 2.4.3) is
\f[CR]invalid_path\f[R].
.SS 7. CBOR beyond JSON
.SS Keys that are not text
A map key in CBOR can be any item.
Two ways reach such a key:
.IP \(bu 2
An index selector on a map selects the value under the equal integer
key.
\f[CR]$[1]\f[R] finds the key 1, and \f[CR]$[\-2]\f[R] finds the key
\-2.
This is not an index from the end: a map has no order in a path.
.IP \(bu 2
An EDN literal in brackets is a key: \f[CR]$[h\(aq01\(aq]\f[R] for a
byte string, \f[CR]$[[1, 2]]\f[R] for an array, \f[CR]$[1.5]\f[R],
\f[CR]$[true]\f[R], \f[CR]$[1000(\(dqx\(dq)]\f[R] for a tag,
\f[CR]$[{\(dqa\(dq: 1}]\f[R] for a map.
This holds at compile time and at run time.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// {1: \(dqx\(dq, \-2: \(dqy\(dq, h\(aq01\(aq: \(dqz\(dq, [1, 2]: \(dqw\(dq}\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$[1]\(dq>();                   \f[I]// the node of \(dqx\(dq\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$[\-2]\(dq>();                  \f[I]// the node of \(dqy\(dq\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$[h\(aq01\(aq]\(dq>();               \f[I]// the node of \(dqz\(dq\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$[[1, 2]]\(dq>();              \f[I]// the node of \(dqw\(dq\f[R]
l.at_path(*cbor::jsonpath::compile<cbor::lazy>(\(dq$[h\(aq01\(aq]\(dq));  \f[I]// the node of \(dqz\(dq, path at run time\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$[h\(aq01\(aq]\(dq, std::string_view>();      \f[I]// ref, \(dqz\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Keys compare by value (RFC 8949 5.6.1).
So \f[CR]h\(aq01\(aq\f[R] and \f[CR]b64\(aqAQ\(aq\f[R] name the same
key.
A key with a head that is not in preferred form is the same key.
An integer and a float are different keys: \f[CR]$[1.0]\f[R] does not
find the key 1.
.PP
When a map has the same key twice, every singular form takes the first
pair.
A wildcard, a descendant segment and a filter take all pairs.
