.PP
A path passes a tag that it does not read, as a generic decoder does
(RFC 8949 6.1): a step and a typed read go to the content.
A path that ends at the tag gives the node of the whole tagged item, so
the caller reads the tag number.
Tags 2 and 3 are not passed: a typed read into an integer reads the
bignum, and an index on a bignum is \f[CR]not_indexable\f[R].
A map key behind tag 55799 is matched as the key without the tag.
A key behind any other tag, tag 28 included, is a different key:
\f[CR]$.k\f[R] does not find \f[CR]1000(\(dqk\(dq)\f[R].
.PP
A path does not resolve a tag 29.
Only \f[CR]cbor::structs\f[R] decode resolves tags 28 and 29.
.SS Typed arrays
A typed array (RFC 8746) is a tag over a byte string.
The tag gives the type, the sign and the byte order of the elements.
An index reads one element as an integer or a float, and a wildcard, a
slice or a filter gives every element.
A path that ends at the tag reads the whole array.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// {\(dqv\(dq: 69(h\(aq07000800\(aq)}, uint16 little endian [7, 8]\f[R]
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.v[1]\(dq, int>(bytes);                       \f[I]// 8\f[R]
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.v[\-1]\(dq, double>(bytes);                   \f[I]// 8.0\f[R]
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.v[2]\(dq, int>(bytes);                       \f[I]// index_out_of_bounds\f[R]
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.v[1][0]\(dq, int>(bytes);                    \f[I]// not_indexable\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$.v[*]\(dq, cbor::nodelist>();              \f[I]// the nodes of 7 and 8\f[R]
l.at_path<\(dq$.v\(dq, cbor::typed_array>();                \f[I]// tag 69, 4 bytes\f[R]
.EE
.PP
An element of an integer array is an integer, and an element of a float
array is a float of the same width.
An element of a 128\-bit float array (tags 83 and 87) is
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R], because no C++ type here holds it.
The element of a clamped uint8 array (tag 68) is an unsigned integer.
.SS 8. Singular queries
A singular query (RFC 9535 2.3.5.1) has only name selectors and index
selectors, one in each segment, and no descendant segment.
It selects one node at most.
An EDN key in brackets is a name selector with one key, so a path with
it is singular.
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] with a node or a typed result reads only a singular
query.
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] with \f[CR]nodelist\f[R] reads every query.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]static_assert\f[R](cbor::is_singular_query_v<\(dq$.a[0][\(aqb\(aq]\(dq>);
\f[B]static_assert\f[R](cbor::is_singular_query_v<\(dq$[h\(aq01\(aq]\(dq>);
\f[B]static_assert\f[R](!cbor::is_singular_query_v<\(dq$.a[*]\(dq>);
\f[B]static_assert\f[R](!cbor::is_singular_query_v<\(dq$..a\(dq>);
\f[B]static_assert\f[R](cbor::is_valid_path_v<\(dq$..a\(dq>);
\f[B]static_assert\f[R](!cbor::is_valid_path_v<\(dq$.a[\(dq>);
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]is_valid_path_v<Path>\f[R] is true when the grammar accepts the
path.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]is_singular_query_v<Path>\f[R] is true when the path is valid and
singular.
.PP
\f[CR]at_path<Path, T>\f[R] with a path that is not singular does not
compile.
\f[CR]lazy.at_path<Path>()\f[R] with such a path stops at a
\f[CR]static_assert\f[R] that names \f[CR]cbor::nodelist\f[R].
\f[CR]compile<T>(path)\f[R] with such a path and a \f[CR]T\f[R] that is
not \f[CR]nodelist\f[R] gives \f[CR]not_singular_query\f[R].
