.PP
\f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R] comes where the stack of the thread
ends (basics.md, section 5), in the steps that recurse:
.IP \(bu 2
a filter, a group in parentheses, a function call or a literal in the
path is nested deeper than the stack holds;
.IP \(bu 2
a descendant segment goes deeper than the stack holds;
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]clone()\f[R] of a node reads an item that is deeper than the stack
holds.
.PP
A path of child segments does not recurse: it reads any number of
segments.
An item that a typed read only skips can be of any depth.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// built with \-DCBOR_STRING_LENGTH_DEFAULT=2\f[R]
\f[I]// {\(dqlong\(dq: \(dqxyz\(dq, \(dqn\(dq: 1}\f[R]
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.n\(dq, int>(bytes);                       \f[I]// string_length_exceeded, the key \(dqlong\(dq is read\f[R]
\f[I]// {\(dqa\(dq: \(dqxyz\(dq, \(dqn\(dq: 1}\f[R]
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.n\(dq, int>(bytes);                       \f[I]// 1, \(dqxyz\(dq is only skipped\f[R]
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.a\(dq, std::string_view>(bytes);          \f[I]// string_length_exceeded\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The limits count only what a path or a lazy reads or decodes.
This holds for the iterators of \f[CR]elements()\f[R] and
\f[CR]entries()\f[R] too.
A skip stays safe: it jumps over the content of a string, and it stops
with \f[CR]too_little_data\f[R] when the item claims more than the bytes
that are left.
.SS 13. Cost
A path reads the heads from the start of the node to the target.
It does not decode what it passes:
.IP \(bu 2
In a map, it reads each key until the key matches, and skips each value
before.
A skip jumps over the content of strings.
.IP \(bu 2
In an array, \f[CR][i]\f[R] skips the i elements before the target.
So an index is linear in the bytes of the elements before it.
\f[CR][\-1]\f[R] skips all elements except the last.
.IP \(bu 2
After the target, it reads nothing.
.PP
A typed read of a scalar allocates nothing, also through tag 24 and with
a key that is not a text.
A view result from an owner allocates nothing: the owner in the result
is a copy of a \f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R].
The form with an rvalue \f[CR]std::string\f[R] allocates the
\f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R] that holds the string, once.
.PP
A \f[CR]nodelist\f[R] read allocates the nodelist.
A filter with a literal allocates for each child that it tests.
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] and the lazy walk read an element of a typed array as
a small CBOR item that the library writes, and this allocates once for
each step.
.PP
A held lazy is a start point.
A path from a lazy at \f[CR]$.cars\f[R] does not read the bytes before
\f[CR]cars\f[R] again.
Many reads below the same node are cheaper from a lazy at that node.
.PP
A key that is not a text compares by value.
A key that is a map or an array is compared by its items and its pairs.
.SS 14. Threads
A path from a lazy follows the rules of a lazy (basics.md): several
threads read one \f[CR]cbor::lazy\f[R] at the same time.
\f[CR]at_path<Path, T>(encoded)\f[R] with bytes has no shared state.
.SS 15. RFC 9535 and this library
A run\-time \f[CR]nodelist\f[R] query gives the result of the JSONPath
Compliance Test Suite in 694 of 706 cases.
50 of these are valid paths with \f[CR]match()\f[R] or
\f[CR]search()\f[R], and the library refuses them as
\f[CR]invalid_path\f[R].
.PP
The other 12 cases are invalid in RFC 9535 and valid EDN, so a path
accepts them:
.PP
